window.FS={"title":"Walking on Ancient Ground","flipbook":"https://heyzine.com/flip-book/d73a2fe642.html","S":{"tench":["Watkin Tench, A Complete Account of the Settlement at Port Jackson (1793), Project Gutenberg","https://www.gutenberg.org/files/3534/3534-h/3534-h.htm"],"nmaPem":["National Museum of Australia, Defining Moments: a Bidjigal leader of the 1790s","https://www.nma.gov.au/defining-moments/resources/pemulwuy"],"adbPem":["Australian Dictionary of Biography: a Bidjigal leader of the 1790s (J. L. Kohen)","https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/pemulwuy-13147"],"dosPem":["Dictionary of Sydney: a Bidjigal leader of the 1790s","https://dictionaryofsydney.org/entry/pemulwuy"],"nmaPox":["National Museum of Australia, Defining Moments: Smallpox epidemic","https://www.nma.gov.au/defining-moments/resources/smallpox-epidemic"],"sirius":["Wikipedia: HMS Sirius (1786), citing the journals of John Hunter and William Bradley","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Sirius_(1786)"],"rations":["University of Wollongong, First Fleet Stories: Rations","http://firstfleet.uow.edu.au/s_rations.html"],"bennelong":["Dictionary of Sydney: Taking of Colbee and Benalon, Manly Cove, 25 November 1789","https://dictionaryofsydney.org/media/3047"],"terra":["Australian Museum: Terra nullius","https://australian.museum/learn/first-nations/unsettled/recognising-invasions/terra-nullius/"],"poor":["UK Parliament: Poor Law reform","https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/transformingsociety/livinglearning/19thcentury/overview/poorlaw/"],"swing":["English Project Captain Swing: Hampshire timeline of events, 1830","https://www.englishprojectcaptainswing.org/the-history-of-the-captain-swing-riots/hampshire-swing-riots/timeline-of-events"],"hansard45":["Hansard, House of Commons, 1 August 1845: Crushing bones in workhouses","https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1845/aug/01/crushing-bones-in-workhouses"],"hansard46":["Hansard, House of Commons, 5 March 1846: The Andover Union","https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1846/mar/05/the-andover-union"],"rba":["Reserve Bank of Australia: The 1840s depression","https://www.rba.gov.au/publications/rdp/2001/2001-07/1840s-depression.html"],"andro":["They came by ship in 1840: Andromache (transcription of the Victorian shipping lists and the Sydney Gazette, 4 August 1840)","https://www.oocities.org/vic1847/40/and40.html"],"bounty":["Museums of History NSW: Entitlement certificates of persons on bounty ships, 1832–42","https://mhnsw.au/articles/nrs-5314-entitlement-certificates/"],"cooks":["Dictionary of Sydney: First people of the Cooks River (Paul Irish)","https://dictionaryofsydney.org/entry/first_people_of_the_cooks_river"],"mabo":["National Museum of Australia, Defining Moments: Mabo decision","https://www.nma.gov.au/defining-moments/resources/mabo-decision"],"flip":["Walking on Ancient Ground (flipbook)","https://heyzine.com/flip-book/d73a2fe642.html"]},"care":["Cultural care. This book and this app deal with killing, kidnapping, disease and the removal of children. The old records use words of their time, and some of them are hard to read. In December 1790 the colony’s orders included killing men and bringing back their heads, and this app says so plainly where it matters. You can stop at any time. If something upsets you, talk to your teacher or someone you trust.","The book says the Resistor “is an archetypal resistance warrior figure” and “does not claim to speak for clans or living cultural traditions.” This app does not either. It uses only public records, and it points readers to Indigenous historians and authors."],"ack":["From the book: “The author acknowledges the ancestral caretakers of the lands described — the Eora, Bidjigal, Gadigal, and Dharug Nations — and pays respect to Elders past and present.”","The book’s educational disclaimer says the story “is not a substitute for primary sources or works by Indigenous historians and authors,” and that teachers and readers “are strongly encouraged to supplement this story with authentic Indigenous voices and scholarship.” This app follows that advice. It does not replace them."],"content":"This app mentions killings, kidnapping, smallpox and the removal of children, and it names people who have died. Some old words and orders are hard to read.","caseFile":["CASE FILE","Case number: 1788–01 / RESISTOR (from the Detective Hunt Log Book at the back of the book)","Question: Who is the figure the book calls the Resistor, and what do the records say?","Places: Warrane (Port Jackson), Botany Bay, Prospect, the Cooks River","Years in the story: 1788 to about 1842","Official finding: none","Status: Evidence incomplete. Further archive work required. (the Log Book’s own words)","Linked pathways: Thomas Freeman and Thomas Curtis","Witnesses:","  1. The Resistor. The book gives no name and no interview.","  2. Thomas Freeman, clerk in the colony’s store, 1790.","  3. Thomas Curtis, English farm worker, arrives 1840.","  4. Warra, a man named in Thomas Curtis’s chapter.","Evidence held: five damaged fragments, A to E. Names, dates and places are missing."],"summary":["Walking on Ancient Ground is a pick-a-path story. You choose one of three characters: a resistance warrior called the Resistor, a clerk named Thomas Freeman in the starving colony of 1790, or a farm worker named Thomas Curtis who arrives in 1840. At the back, the Detective Hunt Log Book gives five damaged records and asks one question: who is the Resistor, and what do the records say?","This app is a companion to that question. Each character has a witness file with four parts: what the book says about them, what they said, what public records show, and what nobody asked. The clue pages let you work through the five fragments. The board sets statements from the book next to statements from the records, so you can see where they match and where they do not. At the end you write your own finding.","The book does not give one answer, and neither does this app. It tells you what is in the book and what is in the records, and what was written for the story. You decide what the evidence supports."],"readingPath":[["Read the opening pages and choose a character.","Open the book from the link below. Read the introduction and “Choose your character”."],["Follow one path to its end, then open that character’s witness file.","The file stays locked until you say you have finished that story. Nobody checks."],["Work through clues A to E.","They are the five damaged records from the Log Book. Collect clues and score each source."],["Use the board.","Pair statements from the book and from the records that do not fit together. Say whom you trust and write a finding."],["Spot the discrepancies.","Find four places where the book and the records do not agree. Choose a reason for each and write why."],["Write your finding.","Then read what the records can and cannot tell us."]],"witnesses":[{"n":1,"key":"resistor","name":"The Resistor","fileLabel":"Witness File 1","role":"Resistance figure in the story, based on a Bidjigal leader of the 1790s (the book’s words)","teaser":"Never named in the book. He is seen only through the choices you make.","chapter":"The Resistor (printed pages 6 to 22)","box":["WITNESS FILE 1","Name: not given in the book. Called “the Resistor”.","Role: warrior; the book calls the figure “archetypal”","Places: near Botany Bay; the cornfields at Prospect","In the book: printed pages 6 to 22","Words in the book: one whispered sentence","Not asked: why the gamekeeper was speared, in his own words"],"background":["The book says: “You are the Resistor, a legendary warrior from the ancient ridges.” Old wounds show the battles he has survived. A crow flies overhead, always watching and guiding him.","He was born near the great harbour that newcomers called Botany Bay. He led people from the Eora, Dharug and nearby clans against the strangers who arrived by ship in 1788. The book says the strangers claimed the land by terra nullius, and that a great sickness in 1789 devastated the clans.","The book says that years earlier he speared the Governor’s gamekeeper to avenge shootings and kidnappings, and that this started a resistance that made him a legend.","On the night of the story he waits with fifty warriors to attack the cornfields at Prospect. The settlers have taken the hunting grounds and his people do not have enough to eat.","The book’s front pages say the Resistor “is an archetypal resistance warrior figure inspired by the documented historical record of a Bidjigal leader who led a sustained guerrilla campaign in the 1790s.”"],"said":[["Page 11 (Scout the Farm)","“Fire can be a weapon or a way to stay hidden. Tonight, we may need both.” He whispers this to the warrior beside him. It is the only sentence the book gives him."],["Pages 7 to 18","He mostly acts, and the book gives him almost no words. What he does depends on your choices."],["The path ends","The book gives him nine path ends. They are listed in the answers below."]],"records":[{"t":"Watkin Tench wrote that on the night of 9 to 10 December 1790 a sergeant of marines and three convicts went on a shooting party near Botany Bay. One of them was McEntire (also spelled McIntyre), the Governor’s gamekeeper. Tench says a young man “with a speck or blemish on his left eye” threw a spear and wounded him in the left side. Tench also wrote that Colbee and other men from the area gave the name of the man who did it and said he lived at Botany Bay. Tench’s own word for him was “murderer”, which shows whose side the writer was on.","src":["tench"]},{"t":"The National Museum of Australia says a Bidjigal man from the Botany Bay area had “a speck” in one eye and was involved in the wounding of McIntyre on 10 December 1790. It says McIntyre was “feared and hated by the Eora people”, and that the attack may have been payback for breaking Eora law and for violence against First Nations people.","src":["nmaPem"]},{"t":"The Australian Dictionary of Biography says that from 1792 this Bidjigal leader led raids at Prospect, Toongabbie, the Georges River, Parramatta, Brickfield Hill and the Hawkesbury River. It says the raids were made for food, “particularly corn”, or as payback for atrocities. David Collins, who was in the colony, suggested most of the attacks followed the settlers’ “own misconduct”, including the kidnapping of Aboriginal children. The National Museum says the first raid was at Prospect in May 1792.","src":["adbPem","nmaPem"]},{"t":"In March 1797 he led a raid on the government farm at Toongabbie. Settlers and soldiers followed his group to Parramatta. He was wounded by seven pieces of buckshot, was taken to hospital, and escaped with an iron on his leg. By late April 1797 he was seen near Botany Bay, recovered.","src":["adbPem","dosPem"]},{"t":"After those escapes the Darug believed that firearms could not kill him.","src":["adbPem"]},{"t":"The book’s reading list names a National Museum page and a 1987 novel by Eric Willmot about the same leader. The Dictionary of Sydney calls Willmot’s book “a brilliant work of fiction”, so it is a novel built on records and not a record itself.","src":["dosPem"]}],"notAsked":[["Why was the gamekeeper speared?","Every record of the spearing was written by the colony’s officers. Historians suggest a reason, but the records do not say what the people involved said at the time."],["Was there one Resistor, or several leaders?","The book calls the figure archetypal. The records name one man, and they were written by people who were fighting him."],["What did the Bidjigal people say about the raids?","Item E in the Log Book is a later oral account, and the book says the speaker and place are not known. Many oral histories stay under community control, so the app does not go looking for more."],["Which parts of the Resistor’s story come from records and which were written for the story?","The book says some dialogue and thoughts are imagined. Only you can tell which is which by checking the records, and the board is built for that."]],"real":["A gamekeeper named McEntire or McIntyre was speared on 9 to 10 December 1790 (Tench).","Raids on farms at Prospect from 1792, mainly for corn (National Museum; Australian Dictionary of Biography).","A wounding and escape at Parramatta in 1797.","A smallpox epidemic among First Nations people around Sydney in April 1789."],"made":["The night raid at Prospect with fifty warriors and a crow guide.","The meeting with Thomas Freeman across the flames.","The scenes that follow each of your choices, including every path end.","Every spoken line and every thought. The book says dialogue and thoughts have been imagined."]},{"n":2,"key":"freeman","name":"Thomas Freeman","fileLabel":"Witness File 2","role":"Captain’s clerk who stayed in the colony and works in the commissary (the store)","teaser":"Hands out food in a starving colony, and must march with Captain Tench.","chapter":"Thomas Freeman (printed pages 26 to 37)","box":["WITNESS FILE 2","Name: Thomas Freeman","Role: clerk, then store worker in the commissary","Place: Sydney Cove and the bush toward Botany Bay","In the book: printed pages 26 to 37","Questioned: never. The book gives choices and thoughts.","Record found for him: none yet"],"background":["The book says Freeman is the captain’s clerk who came to New South Wales on the Sirius, the flagship of the First Fleet. When he could have gone home to England, he chose to stay and work in the commissary, the colony’s one store. There was nothing for him at home.","The book says the Sirius was wrecked near Norfolk Island “earlier this year”. Now he hands out “mouldy, weevil-filled flour and a few thin slices of salt pork”. Everyone is hungry.","The book says the Eora people and other First Nations call the place Ngurra, their Country. Last December, it says, the Resistor speared Governor Phillip’s gamekeeper. “The soldiers say he cannot be shot.”","Tomorrow, the book says, Freeman must march with Captain Watkin Tench and some marines into the deep gullies and thick bush. He wonders whether the Resistor is “just a man trying to protect his people and his Country”."],"said":[["Page 26 (thinking)","“What if the Resistor is not the savage the officers say he is, but just a man trying to protect his people and his Country?”"],["After page 26","What Freeman says and what is said to him depends on your choices. The lines from the paths are listed in the answers below."]],"records":[{"t":"HMS Sirius was the flagship of the First Fleet. She was wrecked on a reef at Norfolk Island on 19 March 1790 while landing stores. Her crew were stranded until 21 February 1791. Tench’s printed text gives the date of the wreck as “the 19th of February”, so the date needs checking in the original. The ship carried ten guns and was rated “nominally” as a sixth-rate.","src":["sirius","tench"]},{"t":"On 27 March 1790 the colony’s order cut the weekly ration from 1 April to 4 lb of flour, 2½ lb of salt pork and 1½ lb of rice for everyone. After the news of the wreck, a smaller ration began on 12 April: 2½ lb of flour, 2 lb of pork and 2 lb of rice (or a quart of peas) a week. Gamekeepers and fishermen got a small extra issue.","src":["tench","rations"]},{"t":"About the flour, Tench wrote that it was “the remnant of what was brought from the Cape, by the Sirius, and was good”, and that soldiers and convicts boiled it up with greens.","src":["tench"]},{"t":"On 9 to 10 December 1790 the gamekeeper was speared. Governor Phillip ordered an expedition. Tench asked him to change the plan from killing ten men to capturing six. The order as Tench printed it was to bring in six men who lived near the head of Botany Bay or, if that could not be done, to put six to death. The party carried ropes, and hatchets and bags to carry the heads of anyone killed.","src":["tench"]},{"t":"Tench listed his party on the first march, on 14 December 1790: himself, a captain, two lieutenants, two surgeons, three sergeants, three corporals and forty private soldiers. No clerk is named. He wrote that on the first day they walked until four o’clock “without seeing a native”. On the second day they saw five men on a beach, who ran off, found empty huts with only fish spears, and met Colbee, who told them the man they wanted had gone south. A second march on 22 December found the huts empty, and Tench called it a “fruitless peregrination”.","src":["tench"]}],"notAsked":[["Was a clerk ever sent on the march?","Tench lists his party by rank. A commissary clerk is not among them, so the story’s march is an addition and not a record."],["What did the people in the store think of the order to bring back heads?","Tench wrote that he asked Phillip to change it. He does not say what the soldiers, convicts or clerks thought."],["Was there a Thomas Freeman?","No source used for this app names a clerk called Thomas Freeman on the Sirius. A crew list and the colony’s muster have not been checked yet, so this app treats him as a story character."],["Who was asked about the people Freeman might meet in the bush?","Nobody. They are in the story and in no record. The book gives them no names."]],"real":["The Sirius, her wreck at Norfolk Island, the cut ration and Captain Watkin Tench.","The spearing of the Governor’s gamekeeper in December 1790 and the marches that followed.","Other people and events named in Freeman’s paths (see the answers on this page)."],"made":["Thomas Freeman himself, as far as this app has checked.","Freeman’s place on the march, the spear that grazes a marine, and Tench’s order to fire.","Every scene that follows your choices, and every path end.","Every spoken line and every thought."],"image":{"src":"images/raper-sirius-1790.jpg","alt":"A watercolour of the ship Sirius tilted in rough water on a reef off an island. A British flag flies on a pole at the right, and pine trees stand on the shore at the left.","cap":"The melancholy loss of HMS Sirius off Norfolk Island, March 19th 1790. Watercolour by George Raper, who sailed with the First Fleet.","credit":"George Raper (1769–1796). National Library of Australia, nla.pic-an21511971. Via Wikimedia Commons. Public domain."}},{"n":3,"key":"curtis","name":"Thomas Curtis","fileLabel":"Witness File 3","role":"Farm worker from Hampshire who sails to New South Wales in 1840","teaser":"Leaves poverty and the workhouse behind for a new start in New South Wales.","chapter":"Thomas Curtis (printed pages 44 to 49)","box":["WITNESS FILE 3","Name: Thomas Curtis","Role: farm worker; free settler","Home: near Timsbury, Test Valley, Hampshire","Ship: the barque Andromache, 1840","Land: near the Cooks River","In the book: printed pages 44 to 49","Record found for him: a family named Curtis on the Andromache"],"background":["The book says Curtis left England because staying meant ruin. In the Test Valley fields near Timsbury, soldiers home from the wars against Napoleon could not find work, and wages were too low to feed a family. Then threshing machines arrived.","The book says he heard about the workhouse at Andover, where men crushed bones for a bowl of thin gruel, and says the New Poor Law of 1834 had ended outdoor relief for men in his position. Bright posters in Romsey promised cheap land, good wages and a fresh start in New South Wales.","With his wife Mary Ann Pragnell and teenage son Robert he sailed on the barque Andromache for 113 days, stopped briefly at Port Phillip, and sailed into Sydney Harbour in August 1840.","They settled near the Cooks River, south of Sydney. The book says drought came and by 1842 the river had shrunk to muddy pools. One evening he and Robert go west to look for better land and are lost, and a Gadigal man guides them back."],"said":[["Pages 46 to 49","The book gives Curtis no spoken words. What he does depends on your choices. The paths and their ends are listed in the answers below."]],"records":[{"t":"A transcription of the Victorian shipping lists says the 468-ton barque Andromache, Captain Thomas New, sailed from London on 28 February 1840 on a “113 day passage” and arrived at Port Phillip on 24 June 1840 with assisted immigrants under Surgeon-Superintendent Dr George Harvey. Eleven people went on to Sydney, leaving Port Phillip on 29 July and arriving on 2 August 1840. The page lists them as a “Curtis family of 4: Thomas 37 with Mary Ann 29, Robert 16 and Margaret 18”. It cites the Sydney Gazette of 4 August 1840 for the passengers.","src":["andro"]},{"t":"The records for assisted and bounty ships in New South Wales include entitlement certificates, which give each immigrant’s name, age, calling, religion, native place and parents. They have not been opened for this app, so the family’s details are not confirmed.","src":["bounty"]},{"t":"In 1830 workers across Hampshire broke threshing machines. More than seventy towns and villages were affected. On 20 November 1830 a crowd destroyed a factory at Upper Clatford, near Andover.","src":["swing"]},{"t":"The Poor Law Amendment Act of 1834 ended outdoor relief for able-bodied people. In many areas help was given only inside the workhouse, where the regime was “deliberately harsh and often cruel”.","src":["poor"]},{"t":"On 1 August 1845 a member of Parliament raised complaints that people in the Andover workhouse crushed bones and fought over the marrow and scraps of meat on them. A Select Committee looked into the Andover Union in 1846.","src":["hansard45","hansard46"]},{"t":"The Reserve Bank of Australia says there was severe drought in 1838 to 1840, and that it forced the colony to import wheat.","src":["rba"]}],"notAsked":[["Who was Margaret?","The shipping list has a Margaret aged 18 with the Curtis family. The book names only a wife and a son. The list gives Mary Ann as 29 and Robert as 16, which is only 13 years apart, so the list may join different people or contain errors."],["How did Curtis get his land by the Cooks River?","The book says only that he “settled” there. It does not say whether he rented, bought or took the land."],["Did the Romsey posters exist?","The book says bright posters in Romsey promised cheap land and good wages. This app has not found one. The notices shown in the book are labelled reconstructions."],["What did the Gadigal people living near the river think of new farmers?","The book gives only Warra’s actions. No record used here gives their view."]],"real":["The Andromache and her 1840 voyage, and a family named Curtis on the list of people who went on to Sydney. This app has not confirmed it is the same family.","Hampshire’s threshing-machine riots, the 1834 Poor Law, and the Andover bone-crushing inquiry (which came later, in 1845 and 1846).","Drought in New South Wales around 1838 to 1840."],"made":["Everything said about Thomas, Mary Ann and Robert as people. The book says some dialogue and thoughts are imagined.","The evening lost in the bush, the meeting with Warra, and every path end.","Every thought and every gesture."]},{"n":4,"key":"warra","name":"Warra","fileLabel":"Witness File 4","role":"Gadigal man who guides Curtis and Robert back to the river","teaser":"Meets Curtis and his son near the Cooks River. Speaks no words in the book.","chapter":"Thomas Curtis (printed pages 44 to 49)","box":["WITNESS FILE 4","Name: “Warra”, as he “wants to be known”","Role: guide","Place: bush west of the Cooks River, about 1842","Language: no shared language with Curtis","Record found for him: none"],"background":["The book says that as Curtis and his son Robert are lost, “a Gadigal man” steps out of the shadows. He moves without sound and his eyes are steady. He points the way back toward the river and the lights of the settlement. “He wants to be known as Warra.”","The book says that Curtis does not speak his language, nor does he speak Curtis’s."],"said":[["Page 45","Nothing in words. He shows the way back by pointing."],["Pages 46 to 49","What Warra does next depends on your choices. It is listed in the answers below."]],"records":[{"t":"No source used for this app names a man called Warra. The book says he wants to be known by that name, and this app treats him as a story character.","src":[]},{"t":"The Dictionary of Sydney says the upper Cooks River valley seems to have been Wanngal clan land, and the lower reaches were either Cadigal or Gameygal land, or both. It adds that it is hard to be certain which clan a person belonged to or what language they spoke.","src":["cooks"]},{"t":"Aboriginal people kept fishing the Cooks River after 1788. In the 1830s the naturalists James Backhouse and George Walker saw a fishing party of three men and two women speared fish from bark canoes on the river. In 1868 the river’s Aboriginal people were still fishing and had been granted a fishing boat.","src":["cooks"]}],"notAsked":[["Did Warra want to help?","The story is told from Curtis’s side. It does not show what Warra thinks, or whether he wanted Curtis there."],["Where did people live and camp by 1840?","The records show people fishing the river, but say little about where families lived."],["Whose voice should tell this?","The book says it does not speak for clans or living traditions. Public records can show that people were present. They cannot give Warra’s own account."]],"real":["Aboriginal people lived by and fished the Cooks River in the 1830s and 1840s.","Colonists’ records name the Cadigal (Gadigal) and Wanngal clans for this area, with uncertainty about the exact lands."],"made":["Warra, the meeting, and the name he “wants to be known” by.","Every gesture and every outcome."]}],"trustOptions":["The book’s own story text","A writer at the time, such as Tench","A government order or record","A later historian or museum","I cannot tell yet"],"who":{"b":"In the book","r":"In the record"},"boardIntro":"Each question sets statements from the book beside statements from public records. Pair two that do not fit together, say whom you trust, and write what you think the difference means. Then lock in your finding to see what the sources say. Some statements fit well. Some need a check. You decide.","topics":[{"id":"when","gate":2,"title":"When was the gamekeeper speared?","ask":"Freeman’s chapter gives three time clues. Which statements do not fit together?","cards":[["w1","b","Thomas Freeman, p. 26","“Earlier this year, the Sirius was wrecked near Norfolk Island.”"],["w2","b","Thomas Freeman, p. 26","“Last December, the Resistor speared Governor Phillip’s gamekeeper.”"],["w3","b","Thomas Freeman, p. 26","“Tomorrow, you must march with Captain Watkin Tench and some marines…”"],["w4","b","The book’s plates, pp. 19 and 20","Two plates about the shooting party are dated “December 1790”."],["w5","r","Sirius sources","The Sirius was wrecked at Norfolk Island on 19 March 1790."],["w6","r","Tench, A Complete Account","The shooting party went out on 9 December 1790 and the gamekeeper was speared in the night."],["w7","r","Tench, A Complete Account","Tench’s first march set out on 14 December 1790, five days after the shooting party."]],"pair":["w2","w3"],"reveal":["The records agree with each other. The Sirius was wrecked in March 1790. The gamekeeper was speared on the night of 9 to 10 December 1790. Tench marched on 14 December 1790.","In Freeman’s chapter, “earlier this year” for the wreck and “tomorrow” for the march both fit December 1790. “Last December” does not, because the record puts the spearing only days before the march, in the same month and year.","The book’s own plates for the shooting party are dated December 1790, so the plates and the chapter do not agree either."],"check":"This is on the list of items to check with the author. The app has not changed the book.","src":["sirius","tench","nmaPem"]},{"id":"march","gate":2,"title":"What happened on the march with Tench?","ask":"Compare Freeman’s march with Tench’s own account.","cards":[["m1","b","Thomas Freeman, p. 27","“You march with Captain Tench, hoping to capture the Resistor and earn some favour.”"],["m2","b","Thomas Freeman, p. 27","“…as you trudge through the scrub with fifty men.”"],["m3","b","Thomas Freeman, p. 27","“Then a spear slices through the air, grazing a marine’s arm. Tench shouts the order to fire.”"],["m4","r","Tench, 14 December 1790","His party was himself, a captain, two lieutenants, two surgeons, three sergeants, three corporals and forty private soldiers. No clerk is named."],["m5","r","Tench, December 1790","The order was to bring in six men from near the head of Botany Bay or, if that could not be done, to put six to death. The party carried hatchets and bags for the heads."],["m6","r","Tench, 14 and 15 December 1790","On the first day they saw no one. On the second they saw five men on a beach, who ran off. No fight is recorded."],["m7","r","Tench, December 1790","Tench asked Governor Phillip to change the plan from killing ten men to capturing six, and Phillip agreed."]],"pair":["m3","m6"],"reveal":["The size of the party, about fifty, matches the book, and Tench was in command.","The records do not match the fight. Tench wrote that his party saw no one on the first day, and that five men on the second day ran away. He does not record a spear, a wounded marine or an order to fire. The book’s clerk is also not in Tench’s list of the party.","The records also show what the march was for. It was ordered to capture or kill six men and to bring back their heads. The book says the march is to capture the Resistor.","Tench says he argued for the capture plan. Phillip’s first order to kill ten men is known because Tench wrote about it."],"check":null,"src":["tench"]},{"id":"prospect","gate":1,"title":"Where and when were the Prospect raids?","ask":"The book has the Resistor already leading raids when Freeman’s chapter starts. Check the dates.","cards":[["p1","b","The Resistor, p. 6","“You are getting ready to attack the cornfields at Prospect.”"],["p2","b","Thomas Freeman, p. 26","“He leads raids, burns crops, and spears cattle.”"],["p3","b","The Resistor, p. 6","“Years ago, you speared the Governor’s gamekeeper to avenge shootings and kidnappings.”"],["p4","b","The Resistor, p. 7","“In the bright light of the fire, you see Thomas Freeman watching from a rise with his musket lowered.”"],["p5","r","National Museum of Australia","The raids led by a Bidjigal man began in 1792. The first was at Prospect in May 1792."],["p6","r","Australian Dictionary of Biography","Raids were made for food, “particularly corn”, or as payback for atrocities. Collins suggested most followed the settlers’ “own misconduct”, including kidnapping of Aboriginal children."]],"pair":["p2","p5"],"reveal":["The records put the first raid at Prospect in May 1792, a year and a half after Tench’s marches in December 1790. Freeman’s chapter, which fits December 1790, already says the Resistor “leads raids, burns crops, and spears cattle”.","The records and the book agree on the reasons: food, especially corn, and payback for harm, including the kidnapping of children. The ADB quotes David Collins on this, and Collins lived in the colony.","No record shows a store clerk at a Prospect raid. The meeting across the flames is in the story only."],"check":"The three characters live in different years, and the book joins them. That is the book’s plan, but the order of events differs from the records.","src":["nmaPem","adbPem"]},{"id":"shot","gate":1,"title":"Could he be shot?","ask":"Who said he could not be shot, and when?","cards":[["s1","b","Thomas Freeman, p. 26","“The soldiers say he cannot be shot.”"],["s2","b","The Resistor, p. 6","“Surviving many wounds showed this legendary spirit to others.”"],["s3","r","Australian Dictionary of Biography","After his escapes in 1797, the Darug believed that firearms could not kill him."],["s4","r","ADB and Dictionary of Sydney","In March 1797 he was wounded by seven pieces of buckshot, was taken to hospital and escaped with an iron on his leg."]],"pair":["s1","s3"],"reveal":["In the story, the soldiers say it in about December 1790. In the records, the belief is the Darug people’s and it follows the escapes of 1797, six years later.","The records show he could be wounded. The belief that bullets could not harm him came from surviving. It does not say he could not be shot.","The wounds the book mentions are not dated. Some records, such as 1797, are known. Whether the book means those is not stated."],"check":null,"src":["adbPem","dosPem"]},{"id":"store","gate":2,"title":"What was in the store?","ask":"Freeman hands out flour and pork. What do the records say about them?","cards":[["t1","b","Thomas Freeman, p. 26","“Now you hand out rations of mouldy, weevil-filled flour and a few thin slices of salt pork…”"],["t2","b","The book’s plate, p. 38","“Ration schedule, 12 April 1790. Facsimile-style reconstruction from Tench, Complete Account… Not an original commissary leaf.”"],["t3","r","Tench, A Complete Account","From 12 April 1790 the weekly ration was 2½ lb of flour, 2 lb of pork and 2 lb of rice (or a quart of peas)."],["t4","r","Tench, A Complete Account","Our flour “was the remnant of what was brought from the Cape, by the Sirius, and was good.”"],["t5","r","Tench, A Complete Account","A small extra issue of flour and pork went to the gamekeepers, who hunted for the colony."]],"pair":["t1","t4"],"reveal":["The ration figures in the book’s plate come from Tench and are correct as far as the app can check. Rice was part of the ration. The book’s chapter mentions flour and pork but not rice.","Tench wrote that the Cape flour was good. The book says the flour was mouldy and weevil-filled. This app has found only Tench on the quality of the flour in April 1790. Later months, other diaries and the commissary’s books have not been checked.","The record that gamekeepers had an extra issue links the store to the gamekeeper in the story."],"check":"The flour could have gone bad later in 1790. This is an open question.","src":["tench","rations"]},{"id":"terra","gate":1,"title":"Was the land “terra nullius” in 1788?","ask":"Check what the words mean and when they came into use.","cards":[["n1","b","The Resistor, p. 6","“They claimed the land using the doctrine of terra nullius, pretending no one had cared for it before.”"],["n2","b","Thomas Freeman, p. 26","“For thousands of years, they have fished the rivers, hunted in the forests, and cared for the land. The British acted as if no one lived here.”"],["n3","r","Australian Museum","Terra nullius “is today used as a catch-all phrase to explain how Australia was founded”."],["n4","r","Australian Museum","Governor Bourke’s proclamation of 1835 put the legal principle of terra nullius into Australian law, 47 years after the First Fleet."],["n5","r","National Museum of Australia","Governor Phillip was instructed to treat the “Indians” well, to “conciliate their affections” and keep friendly relations."],["n6","r","Australian Museum","The High Court’s Mabo (No. 2) decision of 3 June 1992 overturned terra nullius."]],"pair":["n1","n4"],"reveal":["The Australian Museum says the legal principle was put into law in 1835, not 1788, and that the words are today used as a catch-all for how Australia was founded.","The book’s idea, that the British acted as if no one had cared for the land, is how many people describe 1788 today. This app has not found Phillip using those words.","Phillip’s written instructions told him to treat the people well. In December 1790 he also ordered the march described in the question about Tench. Both are in the records."],"check":"The words “terra nullius” are used in the book’s account of 1788. Whether to keep them is a decision for the author.","src":["terra","nmaPem","mabo"]},{"id":"andover","gate":3,"title":"What did Curtis know about Andover?","ask":"Check when the Andover stories became public.","cards":[["d1","b","Thomas Curtis, p. 44","“Villagers talked with dread about Andover — the cold red-brick building where families were separated and men, like you, were forced to crush rotting bones…”"],["d2","b","Thomas Curtis, p. 44","“The New Poor Law of 1834 had ended outdoor relief for men in your situation.”"],["d3","b","Thomas Curtis, p. 44","He sails on the Andromache and arrives in Sydney in August 1840."],["d4","r","UK Parliament","The 1834 Poor Law Amendment Act ended outdoor relief for the able-bodied."],["d5","r","Hansard, 1 August 1845","A member of Parliament raised the practice of paupers crushing bones in the Andover workhouse and gnawing the marrow."],["d6","r","Hansard, 5 March 1846","A guardian and magistrate examined ten paupers. All but two said they ate marrow and gristle from the bones."],["d7","r","English Project Captain Swing","In November 1830 a crowd destroyed a factory at Upper Clatford, near Andover."]],"pair":["d1","d5"],"reveal":["The 1834 Poor Law and the 1830 riots match the book’s account of why men like Curtis were afraid of the workhouse.","The records for the bone crushing are from 1845 and 1846, after Curtis sails in 1840. This app has found no earlier source. Villagers could have talked about it earlier, but no record shows it.","The book’s “rotting bones” matches the 1846 evidence, which says some bones had lain in the yard for two or three months. “Red-brick” and “families separated” have not been checked."],"check":"The first public report found is 1845, so talk before 1840 would need another source.","src":["poor","hansard45","hansard46","swing"]},{"id":"ship","gate":3,"title":"Who landed with Curtis?","ask":"Compare the book’s family with the shipping list.","cards":[["h1","b","Thomas Curtis, p. 44","“You, your wife Mary Ann Pragnell, and your teenage son Robert boarded the barque Andromache and sailed for 113 days…”"],["h2","b","Thomas Curtis, p. 44","“After a brief stop at Port Phillip, the ship finally turned north and sailed into Sydney Harbour in August 1840.”"],["h3","r","Shipping lists (transcription)","The Andromache sailed from London on 28 February 1840 and reached Port Phillip on 24 June 1840, a “113 day passage”."],["h4","r","Shipping lists (transcription)","Eleven passengers left Port Phillip on 29 July and arrived in Sydney on 2 August 1840."],["h5","r","Shipping lists (transcription)","“Curtis family of 4: Thomas 37 with Mary Ann 29, Robert 16 and Margaret 18.”"]],"pair":["h1","h5"],"reveal":["The ship, the stop at Port Phillip, the length of the passage and the arrival in August 1840 all match the book.","The list has four Curtises, and the book has three. The book does not mention Margaret. The page gives no maiden names, so Pragnell cannot be checked.","Mary Ann is listed as 29 and Robert as 16. That is only 13 years apart, so the listing may join different people. The dates 28 February to 24 June are also 117 days, not 113. The page does not explain the difference.","This page is a transcription. The original entitlement certificates, which list age and parents, have not been checked."],"check":"Check the certificates in the State archives before saying this family is the book’s family.","src":["andro","bounty"]},{"id":"river","gate":3,"title":"Which people lived by the Cooks River?","ask":"The book names a Gadigal man. What do the records say about the people of this river?","cards":[["r1","b","Thomas Curtis, p. 44","“You settled near the Cooks River, south of Sydney, on land that once belonged to the Gadigal people.”"],["r2","b","Thomas Curtis, p. 45","“A Gadigal man… He wants to be known as Warra.”"],["r3","r","Dictionary of Sydney","The upper valley seems to have been Wanngal land. The lower reaches were Cadigal or Gameygal land, or both."],["r4","r","Dictionary of Sydney","It is “difficult to be certain” which clan a person belonged to or what language they spoke."],["r5","r","National Museum of Australia","Bidjigal Country “stretched from Botany Bay south of the Cooks River and west along the Georges River”."],["r6","r","Dictionary of Sydney","Aboriginal people kept fishing the river through the 1830s and into the 1860s."]],"pair":["r1","r3"],"reveal":["The records do not contradict the book, but they are less certain. The source says the upper river was Wanngal land and the lower river was Cadigal or Gameygal land, and that exact clan lands are hard to know.","Aboriginal people were fishing this river in the 1830s and later, so a man fishing and guiding near the river fits the records.","The Bidjigal Country in the records lies south of the river, so it is also close."],"check":"For the author to check with Gadigal and Wangal sources: which clan names to use, and whether “Ngurra” is the right word in the Resistor’s and Freeman’s chapters.","src":["cooks","nmaPem"]}],"cluesIntro":"These are the five damaged records from the Detective Hunt Log Book (printed pages 56 to 61). Names, dates and places have been removed. For each one, tick the clues you can see, note what is missing, and score how far you trust it. Your answers stay on this device.","items":[{"id":"A","title":"Item A: newspaper or report","type":"A colonial newspaper or official report (printed page 57)","text":["THE COLONIAL OBSERVER","Sydney, [DATE REDACTED] — Price 3d.","OUTRAGE ON THE FRONTIER. — Accounts have reached us of continued resistance by the native inhabitants in the district of [REDACTED]. A party of settlers moving stock was attacked near [REDACTED]. One man was wounded and several cattle driven off. The leader of the resistance is said to be a man known to the settlers as [REDACTED], who has for some time organised raids and warned others against the advance of settlement.","Military and settler parties have been sent out. Some reports claim the resistance is weakening; others state that the native groups remain determined to defend their Country. The names of those involved on both sides are not fully known. Further particulars are anxiously awaited."],"clues":[["a1","present","Settlers moving stock were attacked. One man was wounded and several cattle were driven off."],["a2","present","The leader is said to have organised raids and warned others against settlement."],["a3","present","Military and settler parties were sent out."],["a4","missing","The date, the district, the place and the leader’s name are all removed."],["a5","missing","The article says the names of those involved “on both sides” are not fully known."]],"bias":"Written for settlers, who paid 3d. The headline says “outrage”. It calls the people “native inhabitants” and does not ask them anything.","check":"Raids on stock and maize are recorded in the 1790s. David Collins, who lived in the colony, wrote about them, and the Australian Dictionary of Biography quotes him. The fragment does not say which raid or year it describes.","src":["adbPem","nmaPem"]},{"id":"B","title":"Item B: settler letter","type":"A letter from a settler or immigrant (printed page 58)","text":["…we have at last taken up land after the long voyage and the months in the depot. The country is strange to us and the work is hard. There is talk among the older hands of trouble with the Blacks who will not leave their places. Some say they have a leader who moves from camp to camp and warns the people not to give way. I do not know the truth of it. We keep watch at night. I write so that you may know we are yet alive and hope for better seasons…","[Signature partially illegible]   [Date partially lost]"],"clues":[["b1","present","The writer has taken up land after a long voyage and months in a depot."],["b2","present","There is “talk” of a leader who moves from camp to camp and warns people not to give way."],["b3","present","The writer says, “I do not know the truth of it.”"],["b4","missing","The author, the date and the place are missing."],["b5","contradiction","The writer reports talk from “the older hands”. It is hearsay, not what the writer saw."]],"bias":"The word “Blacks” is the writer’s. The letter is private and written to family, which can make it more honest and also more nervous. It is second-hand talk.","check":"Immigrant depots and assisted passages are recorded. The Andromache list on the Curtis file is one example, but this letter has no name to match it to.","src":["andro","bounty"]},{"id":"C","title":"Item C: military or police report","type":"An official extract about “resistance” or “dispersal” (printed page 59)","text":["EXTRACT FROM OFFICIAL REPORT","Date: [illegible]   District: [REDACTED]","A party under [REDACTED] proceeded to the place where the natives were said to be gathered.","Resistance was offered. Several of the natives were killed or wounded; the remainder dispersed into the bush.","The leader known as [REDACTED] was not among those taken. He is reported to have escaped and to be still at large.","Settlers in the district request further protection. Stock continues to be driven off in small numbers.","— End of recoverable extract —"],"clues":[["c1","present","A party went to a place where “the natives were said to be gathered”."],["c2","present","“Several of the natives were killed or wounded; the remainder dispersed.”"],["c3","present","The leader escaped and is “still at large”."],["c4","missing","The date, the district, the party’s leader and the number killed are missing."],["c5","contradiction","“Resistance was offered” and “dispersed” describe an attack as a defence. The report does not say who fired first."]],"bias":"It is an official report by the party that did the shooting. “Dispersed” is a plain-sounding word for people who were killed or driven off. The settlers’ request for protection is recorded, and nothing is recorded from the people who were attacked.","check":"Similar events are recorded. In March 1797 at Parramatta settlers and soldiers fired on a group, and at least five First Nations men were killed. The fragment does not name that event or any other.","src":["nmaPem","dosPem"]},{"id":"D","title":"Item D: immigration note","type":"A note about why people left Britain and what they found (printed page 60)","text":["Many of those now arriving under the assisted schemes left behind the workhouse, low wages and the enclosure of common land. They were told of good land and opportunity in the colony. On arrival some found the reality harder than the promises. The land was already occupied. Conflict over grass, water and living places was common. The stories of resistance by the native people were known even in the immigrant depots, though few of the new arrivals understood the full meaning of what they heard."],"clues":[["d1","present","People left the workhouse, low wages and the enclosure of common land."],["d2","present","They were promised good land. On arrival it “was already occupied”."],["d3","present","Conflict over grass, water and living places was common."],["d4","present","Stories of resistance were known even in the immigrant depots."],["d5","missing","There is no author, date or place, and no figures."]],"bias":"It looks like the view of a later writer or an official. It speaks for new arrivals (“few … understood”) and says nothing about the land’s First Nations people except as “native people”.","check":"The 1834 Poor Law and the Hampshire riots of 1830 are recorded and match the first lines. This app has found no record of a single note like this one.","src":["poor","swing"]},{"id":"E","title":"Item E: later oral account","type":"A later-recorded recollection from a First Nations perspective (printed page 61)","text":["The old people used to tell of the time when the strangers kept coming onto Country and the people stood against them. There was a leader who moved between the camps and told the people not to give up their places. Some of the strangers were afraid; others came with guns. Many of our people were killed. The story of that resistance is still known. The places where it happened are still remembered.","[Later-recorded oral testimony. Exact speaker and precise location details incomplete in the surviving notes.]"],"clues":[["e1","present","A leader moved between the camps and told people not to give up their places."],["e2","present","Some strangers were afraid. Others came with guns. Many people were killed."],["e3","present","The story is “still known” and the places are “still remembered”."],["e4","missing","The speaker, the place and the date of recording are not known."],["e5","contradiction","It was written down many years after the events. The book says to treat it with care about transmission, memory and framing."]],"bias":"It is the only item from a First Nations perspective, and it is the only one with no name, date or place. The book notes that many oral histories stay within community control. Use only material that has been shared publicly.","check":"This app does not add other oral accounts. The book suggests the Frontier Conflict database, the State Library of Queensland’s First Nations collections and AIATSIS collections, and says to check access conditions and protocols first.","src":[]}],"hunt":[["Tench, A Complete Account of the Settlement at Port Jackson","Published 1793. Written by a marine officer who was in the colony. Primary source.","tench"],["Hansard, House of Commons, 1 August 1845 and 5 March 1846","Official record of debates and evidence. Primary source.","hansard45"],["Hansard, 5 March 1846: The Andover Union","Evidence by a guardian and magistrate. Primary source.","hansard46"],["Shipping lists and the Sydney Gazette of 4 August 1840","A transcription of lists and a newspaper. The original newspaper is a primary source. The transcription is not.","andro"],["National Museum of Australia: Defining Moments page on a Bidjigal leader of the 1790s","A museum page based on historians. Quotes Governor King’s orders. Secondary source.","nmaPem"],["Australian Dictionary of Biography: article on a Bidjigal leader of the 1790s","A reference article that quotes David Collins. Secondary source.","adbPem"],["Dictionary of Sydney: entries on a Bidjigal leader of the 1790s and on the Cooks River","Reference entries. Secondary source.","dosPem"],["Museums of History NSW: Entitlement certificates","A guide to the records of bounty ships from 1832 to 1842. Use it to find the original certificate.","bounty"]],"huntNote":"The book asks you to find at least three independent primary sources that are not in the Log Book, and to put at least one of a different type. The first two rows are primary sources. Check each against the fragments, score it for reliability, and say why. Good places to search are Trove (newspapers), State Library catalogues, the National Archives, early colonial reports, immigration records and publicly shared First Nations oral history material. Check access conditions before you use it.","readNext":["From the book’s own reading list: Watkin Tench (1789 and 1793), David Collins (An Account of the English Colony in New South Wales), Arthur Phillip (1789); James L. Kohen, Aboriginal Environmental Impacts (UNSW Press, 1995); Eric Willmot’s 1987 novel about a Bidjigal leader (the Dictionary of Sydney calls it a work of fiction); and a National Museum of Australia page on the same man. The titles are in the book’s reading list and are not repeated here, so they do not give away the case.","The book says readers and teachers should seek out works by Indigenous historians and authors. This app does not replace them."],"questions":[{"h":"Before you read: sources","q":["Who wrote each of the five fragments, and who did they write it for?","Which fragments were written at the time? Which were written later?","What does “redacted” mean? Who might remove a name from a record?"]},{"h":"The Resistor","q":["The book says the Resistor is “archetypal”. What is an archetype, and why might a writer use one instead of a named person?","Which parts of the Resistor’s story match the records? Which do not?","The records call him a “murderer” and an “enemy”. Whose words are those? What other words could describe him?"]},{"h":"Thomas Freeman","q":["Freeman’s choices lead to different ends. Which choice would you make, and why?","Tench wrote that he asked Phillip to change an order. What does that tell you about Tench? What does it not tell you?","If a person in a story is not named in the records you have found, does that mean he did not exist?"]},{"h":"Thomas Curtis","q":["Why did the Curtis family leave England? Which records support the book’s reasons?","The book gives Warra no words. What can a story tell us about a person who has no words, and what can it not tell us?","What would you want to ask Warra? Who is the right person to answer?"]},{"h":"Comparing records","q":["Pick one clash from the Board. Which side do you trust more, and why?","Why is it important to keep what a story says apart from what the records say?","Whose voices are missing from the records you have seen? How could you find out more safely?"]}],"teacherNotes":["There is no single answer key for the discussion questions. The book does not give one answer, and neither does this app. The plot endings, the Resistor’s identity and what the records show are behind the “Show answers” toggles, which are closed until you open them.","Spot the Discrepancy shows students how historians compare sources. See the teacher note on that page and in Class mode.","The book asks teachers to supplement the story with Indigenous voices and scholarship. Please follow your school’s protocols, and consider inviting a local Aboriginal education worker or Elder.","The story and the records include killing and the removal of children. Preview the Resistor, Freeman and Board pages before class, and offer students the choice to stop.","Nothing students type leaves their device. Printing is the way to share work."],"classIntro":"These questions go with the book’s Detective Hunt Log Book. They ask students to check sources, notice silences and say how sure they are.","writing":{"title":"Writing task: a source check","time":"About 20 minutes. Aim for 150 to 250 words in total.","steps":["Choose one clash from the Board.","Name the source in the book, and the record you compared it with.","Say what the two agree on and what they do not.","Say what is still missing, and who could fill the gap."],"starters":["The book says…","The record says…","They agree that…","They do not agree that…","I trust ___ more because…","I would want to know…"],"fields":[["claim","The book says…",false],["record","The record says…",false],["agree","Where they agree and where they do not",true],["missing","What is still missing",true]]},"credits":[["Thomas Freeman file","The melancholy loss of HMS Sirius off Norfolk Island, March 19th 1790. Watercolour by George Raper. National Library of Australia, nla.pic-an21511971, via Wikimedia Commons. Public domain."]],"openChecks":["Freeman’s chapter says the gamekeeper was speared “last December” but also says the march with Tench is “tomorrow”. The records put both in December 1790, and the book’s plates are dated December 1790.","The Sirius was wrecked on 19 March 1790 in the Sirius sources. The Project Gutenberg text of Tench gives “19th of February”. The original should be checked.","The book says the flour was mouldy and weevil-filled. Tench wrote that the Cape flour was good. Other sources have not been checked.","No clerk is listed in Tench’s party. The march, the spear and the order to fire are written for the story.","The records put the first raid at Prospect in May 1792. Freeman’s chapter already says the Resistor leads raids and burns crops.","The records say the Darug believed he could not be shot after his escapes in the 1790s. The book says the soldiers believed it in 1790.","The records say terra nullius became law in 1835. The book uses the words for 1788.","Curtis’s ship, the stop at Port Phillip and August 1840 match a shipping list. The list also has a Margaret aged 18, and ages that may be wrong. The certificates have not been opened.","The first record of the Andover bone crushing found is from 1845. The book has villagers talking about it before 1840.","The RBA puts the severe drought in 1838 to 1840. The book’s chapter has the river shrinking by 1842.","For the author and Gadigal and Wangal sources: the clan names for the Cooks River and the word “Ngurra”.","Whether to name the Bidjigal leader in this app. The book does not. The app names him only after a reader locks in a finding, and in the closed teacher box. Tench’s own account, the book’s reading list and the National Museum page name him. The book’s other companion app avoids names of Aboriginal people who have died, so this is the author’s choice.","The book’s plates are labelled facsimile reconstructions. This app does not reuse them.","The app uses the book’s printed page numbers (the flipbook’s page counter runs differently)."],"findingIntro":"This is the page the Log Book asks for: your solved case. Write it from the evidence, and use only what is in the book and the records. Nobody checks it, and it stays on this device.","statusOptions":["One person: the records support one named leader behind the Resistor","A mix: the story joins several people and events","Not decided: the evidence does not settle it","Something else (explain below)"],"sureOptions":["Very sure","Fairly sure","Not very sure","Not sure at all"],"findingReveal":["The book does not say who the Resistor is. It says the figure is inspired by a documented Bidjigal leader of the 1790s, and that it does not claim to speak for clans or living cultural traditions.","What the records support: a Bidjigal man from the Botany Bay area was named by Colbee and others to Tench in December 1790 as the man who speared the Governor’s gamekeeper. Tench spelled his name “Pimelwi”. The National Museum, the Australian Dictionary of Biography and the Dictionary of Sydney name him Pemulwuy. Raids at Prospect and Toongabbie, wounds and an escape in 1797, and a death in 1802 are recorded. The book’s Resistor shares the gamekeeper, the Prospect raids, the wounds and the belief that he cannot be shot.","Later records: the Darug believed firearms could not kill him after 1797. On 1 May 1801 Governor King ordered that Aboriginal people near Parramatta, the Georges River and Prospect could be shot on sight. In November 1801 Pemulwuy was outlawed and rewards were offered. He was shot dead on or just before 2 June 1802, and the National Museum says it is not certain who shot him. A later dispatch from London said that cruelty toward First Nations people would be punished.","What does not match: the order of events (the story joins 1790, 1792 and 1797 into one time), the soldiers’ belief in 1790, Freeman on the march and at Prospect, the fight on the march, and the details of the night raid. Those are the story’s.","What the records cannot tell us: why the gamekeeper was speared in the words of the people involved, what the Bidjigal people said about the raids, and what Warra thought. Almost every record was written by the colony’s officers, who were fighting him. Item E is the only fragment from another point of view, and its speaker and place are not known.","So the case stays open. A careful finding says what the sources show, what the story adds, and what is missing. If your finding does that, it fits the book’s own rule: do not invent facts that cannot be supported by primary evidence."],"about":"This is a companion app for Walking on Ancient Ground by Robin McKean, Perth, Western Australia. It is for readers 12 and older. Read the book first. Open it here:","teacher":{"files":{"1":[{"h":"The Resistor: how the nine paths end (from the book)","p":["Lead the Raid, then Signal Your Warriors to Retreat: “Successful Strike”. The fields burn and the people will have food.","Lead the Raid, then Approach Freeman Alone: “Tentative Connection”. The Resistor and Freeman look at each other and he feels a glimmer of understanding.","Lead the Raid, then Order a Warrior to Strike Him: “Escalated Conflict”. A spear is thrown at Freeman, who hides behind a tree.","Scout the Farm, then Attack Guards: “Heroic Rescue”. The guards fall, one warrior is killed by a stray musket ball, and the children are brought home.","Scout the Farm, then Wait and Burn the Fields: “Planned Strike”. He burns only part of the crop and his name begins to spread.","Scout the Farm, then Retreat and Plan a Bigger Raid: “Tactics and Patience”. He returns weeks later with more warriors and the Prospect farm burns.","Send a Warrior to Parley, then Launch the Raid: “Fiery Retribution”. The fields burn and he wonders if the violence will ever end.","Send a Warrior to Parley, then Pull Back: “Wise Withdrawal”. He raids in small ways and his people endure.","Send a Warrior to Parley, then Send an Elder: “Betrayed Trust”. A musket is fired, the elder falls, and there is nothing left but war."]},{"h":"The Resistor: scenes written for the story","p":["The children held in a hut and their rescue, and the elder shot at a parley while wearing a possum-skin cloak, have no record behind them in this app’s sources."]},{"h":"Who the Resistor is in the records","p":["The book does not name him. The records this app used name the man as Pemulwuy, a Bidjigal man from the Botany Bay area. Tench spelled the name “Pimelwi” when he wrote that Colbee and others named the man who speared the gamekeeper McEntire in December 1790. The National Museum of Australia, the Australian Dictionary of Biography and the Dictionary of Sydney use the name Pemulwuy.","The records from 1798 onwards: the Darug believed firearms could not kill him after his escapes. On 1 May 1801 Governor King ordered that Aboriginal people near Parramatta, the Georges River and Prospect could be shot on sight. In November 1801 he was outlawed and rewards were offered. He was shot dead on or just before 2 June 1802. The National Museum says it is not certain who shot him, and that a later dispatch from London said cruelty toward First Nations people would be punished."],"src":["tench","nmaPem","adbPem","dosPem"]}],"2":[{"h":"Thomas Freeman: how the eight paths end (from the book)","p":["March, then Fire into the Trees: “Survival with Regret”. He fires high on purpose. Tench says “Well done, lad!” and he gets extra rations.","March, then Charge Forward: “Hollow Victory”. A spear cuts his side, the Resistor slips away, he is made an overseer and his wound festers.","March, then Drop Your Musket and Call for a Truce: “Act of Resistance”. Tench’s men call him “Traitor”, put him in irons and lock him in his hut.","Lag Behind, then Follow the Warrior’s Gesture: “Unexpected Mercy”. A warrior leads him out of the bush and he avoids the hunting patrols afterwards.","Lag Behind, then Raise the Musket: “Fearful Escape”. Tench calls him “Deserter” and puts irons on his wrists.","Slip Away, then Stay and Keep Talking: “A Small Bridge”. He talks to a Bidjigal woman gathering yams about the land and about Bennelong.","Slip Away, then Hide in the Bushes: “Lost Chance”. He returns to the settlement and says nothing.","Slip Away, then Run Back to the Colony: “Safe but Unchanged”."]},{"h":"Thomas Freeman: records about a person in one path","p":["Bennelong and Colebee were seized at Manly Cove on 25 November 1789 on Governor Phillip’s order. Bennelong stayed a captive until he escaped in May 1790.","The cave, the warrior who points to a path and the woman gathering yams are written for the story. No record in this app’s sources shows them."],"src":["bennelong"]}],"3":[{"h":"Thomas Curtis: how the four paths end (from the book)","p":["Ask Warra to Help You Understand the Land: “Beginning to See”. Warra shows him the trees, the water and the plants, and Robert learns names in the Gadigal language.","Thank Him Awkwardly and Let Him Go: “Missed Opportunity”. Curtis keeps farming the English way and the land keeps resisting him. The book says the Romsey posters never mentioned that the land already had its own people.","Gesture That You Would Like to Fish with Him: “Shared Waters”. Warra shows him how to stand in the shallows of the Cooks River and they share the catch.","Find a Simple Way to Thank Him: “Small Thanks, Lasting Change”. Curtis offers damper and a tin cup of tea, Warra accepts, and Curtis starts to notice the land differently."]}],"4":[{"h":"Warra: what he does on each path (from the book)","p":["If Curtis asks for help: Warra studies him, nods, and shows which trees flower at which time, how the ground near the river keeps water in drought, and which plants are safe to eat or use as medicine.","If Curtis thanks him awkwardly: Warra turns and disappears into the bush.","If Curtis mimes fishing: Warra walks to a bend in the river, shows how to stand still in the shallows, points out where the fish hide and shares the catch.","If Curtis offers damper and tea: Warra accepts them “without ceremony”, gives “a single nod” and walks back into the bush."]}]},"topics":{"shot":[{"h":"Could he be shot? The records that are held back","p":["After his escapes in 1797 the Darug believed that firearms could not kill him. On 1 May 1801 Governor King ordered that Aboriginal people near Parramatta, the Georges River and Prospect could be shot on sight. In November 1801 he was outlawed and rewards were offered for his capture or death.","He was shot dead on or just before 2 June 1802. The National Museum says it is not certain who shot him."],"src":["adbPem","nmaPem"]}]},"all":[{"h":"The Resistor: how the nine paths end (from the book)","p":["Lead the Raid, then Signal Your Warriors to Retreat: “Successful Strike”. The fields burn and the people will have food.","Lead the Raid, then Approach Freeman Alone: “Tentative Connection”. The Resistor and Freeman look at each other and he feels a glimmer of understanding.","Lead the Raid, then Order a Warrior to Strike Him: “Escalated Conflict”. A spear is thrown at Freeman, who hides behind a tree.","Scout the Farm, then Attack Guards: “Heroic Rescue”. The guards fall, one warrior is killed by a stray musket ball, and the children are brought home.","Scout the Farm, then Wait and Burn the Fields: “Planned Strike”. He burns only part of the crop and his name begins to spread.","Scout the Farm, then Retreat and Plan a Bigger Raid: “Tactics and Patience”. He returns weeks later with more warriors and the Prospect farm burns.","Send a Warrior to Parley, then Launch the Raid: “Fiery Retribution”. The fields burn and he wonders if the violence will ever end.","Send a Warrior to Parley, then Pull Back: “Wise Withdrawal”. He raids in small ways and his people endure.","Send a Warrior to Parley, then Send an Elder: “Betrayed Trust”. A musket is fired, the elder falls, and there is nothing left but war."]},{"h":"The Resistor: scenes written for the story","p":["The children held in a hut and their rescue, and the elder shot at a parley while wearing a possum-skin cloak, have no record behind them in this app’s sources."]},{"h":"Who the Resistor is in the records","p":["The book does not name him. The records this app used name the man as Pemulwuy, a Bidjigal man from the Botany Bay area. Tench spelled the name “Pimelwi” when he wrote that Colbee and others named the man who speared the gamekeeper McEntire in December 1790. The National Museum of Australia, the Australian Dictionary of Biography and the Dictionary of Sydney use the name Pemulwuy.","The records from 1798 onwards: the Darug believed firearms could not kill him after his escapes. On 1 May 1801 Governor King ordered that Aboriginal people near Parramatta, the Georges River and Prospect could be shot on sight. In November 1801 he was outlawed and rewards were offered. He was shot dead on or just before 2 June 1802. The National Museum says it is not certain who shot him, and that a later dispatch from London said cruelty toward First Nations people would be punished."],"src":["tench","nmaPem","adbPem","dosPem"]},{"h":"Thomas Freeman: how the eight paths end (from the book)","p":["March, then Fire into the Trees: “Survival with Regret”. He fires high on purpose. Tench says “Well done, lad!” and he gets extra rations.","March, then Charge Forward: “Hollow Victory”. A spear cuts his side, the Resistor slips away, he is made an overseer and his wound festers.","March, then Drop Your Musket and Call for a Truce: “Act of Resistance”. Tench’s men call him “Traitor”, put him in irons and lock him in his hut.","Lag Behind, then Follow the Warrior’s Gesture: “Unexpected Mercy”. A warrior leads him out of the bush and he avoids the hunting patrols afterwards.","Lag Behind, then Raise the Musket: “Fearful Escape”. Tench calls him “Deserter” and puts irons on his wrists.","Slip Away, then Stay and Keep Talking: “A Small Bridge”. He talks to a Bidjigal woman gathering yams about the land and about Bennelong.","Slip Away, then Hide in the Bushes: “Lost Chance”. He returns to the settlement and says nothing.","Slip Away, then Run Back to the Colony: “Safe but Unchanged”."]},{"h":"Thomas Freeman: records about a person in one path","p":["Bennelong and Colebee were seized at Manly Cove on 25 November 1789 on Governor Phillip’s order. Bennelong stayed a captive until he escaped in May 1790.","The cave, the warrior who points to a path and the woman gathering yams are written for the story. No record in this app’s sources shows them."],"src":["bennelong"]},{"h":"Thomas Curtis: how the four paths end (from the book)","p":["Ask Warra to Help You Understand the Land: “Beginning to See”. Warra shows him the trees, the water and the plants, and Robert learns names in the Gadigal language.","Thank Him Awkwardly and Let Him Go: “Missed Opportunity”. Curtis keeps farming the English way and the land keeps resisting him. The book says the Romsey posters never mentioned that the land already had its own people.","Gesture That You Would Like to Fish with Him: “Shared Waters”. Warra shows him how to stand in the shallows of the Cooks River and they share the catch.","Find a Simple Way to Thank Him: “Small Thanks, Lasting Change”. Curtis offers damper and a tin cup of tea, Warra accepts, and Curtis starts to notice the land differently."]},{"h":"Warra: what he does on each path (from the book)","p":["If Curtis asks for help: Warra studies him, nods, and shows which trees flower at which time, how the ground near the river keeps water in drought, and which plants are safe to eat or use as medicine.","If Curtis thanks him awkwardly: Warra turns and disappears into the bush.","If Curtis mimes fishing: Warra walks to a bend in the river, shows how to stand still in the shallows, points out where the fish hide and shares the catch.","If Curtis offers damper and tea: Warra accepts them “without ceremony”, gives “a single nod” and walks back into the bush."]}],"teacherOnlySrc":["bennelong"]},"spot":{"title":"Spot the Discrepancy","intro":["A detective does not only collect clues. A detective also notices when two accounts do not agree, and then works out why.","This activity has four places where the book and the records do not fit, or where two records do not fit. For each one, read both sides, choose the reason you think is most likely, and write why. Then lock in your answer to see what the evidence shows."],"how":["Read both sides of the case. Each side names its source.","Pick the reason you think is most likely.","Write one or two sentences that use the quotes to back up your reason.","Lock in your answer. The app then shows what the evidence points to, and what is still open."],"note":"There may be more than one good reason, and some cases cannot be settled. If the evidence is not enough, “I cannot tell yet” is a proper finding. Use only what is on the page.","reasons":[["story","The author added a witness so readers could see it happen"],["slip","The writer made a slip or remembered it wrongly"],["copy","Someone copied or counted wrongly"],["sources","Good sources disagree with each other"],["wrong","The record could be wrong"]],"teacher":["Spot the Discrepancy shows students how historians compare sources. A historian puts two accounts side by side, asks who wrote each one and when, and asks whether the writer could have known. Then the historian says which reason best fits the evidence, and says plainly when the evidence cannot settle it.","There is no answer key. Each case ends with what the evidence points to and what is still open. When you mark, look for a reason that is backed by something in the quotes, not for a match with the app’s reading.","The book is historical fiction, and the story is allowed to differ from the record. A difference is not a mistake by itself. The activity asks students to tell a story choice from a slip or a counting error.","The four cases are marked as still being checked with the author. The app has not changed the book."],"prompts":["Pick one case. Which source is closer to the event, and does that make it more reliable?","In which case is the difference most likely a choice by the writer, and what in the evidence tells you?","In which case could the record itself be wrong? What would you check to find out?","Choose a case that stays open. What source would you look for next?"],"cases":[{"id":"march","gate":2,"title":"The march with no fight","ask":"In the book, Freeman marches with Captain Tench and a spear is thrown. What does Tench’s own account say?","a":{"kind":"book","who":"Walking on Ancient Ground, Thomas Freeman, p. 27","t":["“You march with Captain Tench, hoping to capture the Resistor and earn some favour.”","“Then a spear slices through the air, grazing a marine’s arm. Tench shouts the order to fire.”"],"src":["flip"]},"b":{"kind":"real","who":"Watkin Tench, march of 14 and 15 December 1790","t":["His party was himself, a captain, two lieutenants, two surgeons, three sergeants, three corporals and forty private soldiers. No clerk is named.","On the first day they walked until four o’clock “without seeing a native”. On the second day they saw five men on a beach, who ran off. No fight is recorded."],"src":["tench"]},"clues":["Who wrote each account? Was the writer on the march?","Does Tench’s list of the party include a clerk?","What does the book say about the dialogue and thoughts in the story?"],"lean":["story"],"reveal":["The book is historical fiction. Its front pages say some dialogue and personal thoughts have been imagined. Straight after the spear, the story gives the reader a choice of what to do, so the fight is part of how the pick-a-path story works. The app marks this scene “From the story”.","Tench led the march and wrote about it. His account has no fight, no spear thrown and no clerk. The app has found no other account that records a fight on this march.","The evidence points to a story choice. Nothing found suggests that Tench made an error, but the app has read only his printed account, so it cannot say that nothing else happened that was not written down."],"open":"The app has not looked for other accounts of the march, such as journals of other men in the party.","status":"made","src":["tench"]},{"id":"december","gate":2,"title":"When was “last December”?","ask":"Freeman’s chapter gives three time clues. Do they all fit the records at once?","a":{"kind":"book","who":"Walking on Ancient Ground, Thomas Freeman, p. 26","t":["“Earlier this year, the Sirius was wrecked near Norfolk Island.”","“Last December, the Resistor speared Governor Phillip’s gamekeeper.”","“Tomorrow, you must march with Captain Watkin Tench and some marines…”"],"src":["flip"]},"b":{"kind":"real","who":"Records of 1790","t":["The Sirius was wrecked at Norfolk Island on 19 March 1790.","The shooting party went out on 9 December 1790 and the gamekeeper was speared in the night. The National Museum gives 10 December.","Tench’s first march set out on 14 December 1790, five days after the shooting party."],"src":["sirius","tench","nmaPem"]},"clues":["Work out the year of the chapter if “tomorrow” is the march of 14 December 1790. Which of the other two clues fit that year?","Now try the year 1791. Which clues fit?","The book’s plates on pages 19 and 20 are dated December 1790. What does that suggest?"],"lean":["slip","story"],"reveal":["If the march is “tomorrow” and the march was on 14 December 1790, then “earlier this year” fits the March wreck. But the spearing was only days before the march, in the same month, so “last December” does not fit.","If the chapter is set in 1791, “last December” would fit December 1790. But then “earlier this year” does not fit a wreck in March 1790, and the march of 14 December 1790 would not be “tomorrow”. No single year makes all three clues fit the records.","This could be a slip in the wording, or the story may have moved events closer together or further apart on purpose. The app’s Board notes that the story joins events from several different years into one time."],"open":"Only the author can say which it is. The app cannot tell a slip from a choice here.","status":"check","src":["sirius","tench","nmaPem"]},{"id":"andromache","gate":3,"title":"How many days on the Andromache?","ask":"The book says 113 days. Does the shipping list agree with the book, and with its own dates?","a":{"kind":"book","who":"Walking on Ancient Ground, Thomas Curtis, p. 44","t":["“You, your wife Mary Ann Pragnell, and your teenage son Robert boarded the barque Andromache and sailed for 113 days…”"],"src":["flip"]},"b":{"kind":"real","who":"Shipping list (transcription of the Victorian shipping lists)","t":["The Andromache “sailed 28-2-1840 from London for a 113 day passage” and “arrived Melbourne 24-6-1840”.","Counting the days from 28 February to 24 June 1840 gives 117. The year 1840 had 29 days in February, and that is included."],"src":["andro"]},"clues":["Does the book’s number match the list’s number?","Count from 28 February to 24 June 1840. Do you get 113?","The list says the ship collected passengers at Plymouth. Does it say what day she left Plymouth?"],"lean":["copy","wrong"],"reveal":["In this case the book and the list agree. Both say 113 days. The mismatch is inside the list, between its “113 day passage” and its own two dates, which are 117 days apart.","This looks like a counting or copying error, either in the list or in the source it came from. But the app cannot rule out that the 113 days were counted from a different starting day. The list says the ship collected passengers at Plymouth and does not give the day she left there. Counting back 113 days from 24 June gives 3 March 1840, four days after 28 February.","The evidence does not show which explanation is right. It does show that the book has copied a number that does not match the dates beside it."],"open":"The page is a transcription. The original shipping records have not been checked, so the app cannot say where the difference began.","status":"check","src":["andro"]},{"id":"sirius","gate":2,"title":"When was the Sirius wrecked?","ask":"Two sources give different dates for the wreck. The book gives no date. Which clues help you decide?","a":{"kind":"real","who":"Watkin Tench, A Complete Account (printed text)","t":["“…we learned, that the ‘Sirius’ had been wrecked on Norfolk Island, on the 19th of February.”","Earlier in the same book: the Sirius carried Major Ross and the convicts to Norfolk Island and “sailed on the 6th of March”.","Tench wrote that he had never been to Norfolk Island and knew it only from what others told him."],"src":["tench"]},"b":{"kind":"real","who":"HMS Sirius (1786), citing the journals of John Hunter and William Bradley","t":["“On 19 March 1790, Sirius was wrecked on a reef at Norfolk Island while landing stores.”"],"src":["sirius"]},"book":"The book (Freeman, p. 26) says only “Earlier this year, the Sirius was wrecked near Norfolk Island.”  It gives no date, so it fits either one.","clues":["Which account was written by someone who was on the island? Which was written by someone who heard the news in Sydney?","Tench says the Sirius sailed on 6 March. Could she be wrecked on 19 February?","The app has used a printed text of Tench. Could the printed text differ from what he wrote?"],"lean":["slip","copy","sources"],"reveal":["The two sources differ by a month. Tench’s date is 19 February and the Sirius page gives 19 March 1790.","There is a clue inside Tench’s own book. He says the Sirius sailed on 6 March, so a wreck on 19 February would be before she left. He also says he knew Norfolk Island only from what others told him, and that he learned of the wreck at Sydney.","These clues point to an error in Tench’s date. It could be his slip, a mistake in an edition, or a mistake in the text the app used. The app cannot tell which."],"open":"The app has not checked an original printing of Tench or the journals of Hunter and Bradley, and has not found a source that explains how historians settle this date. So the sources differ, and 19 March is the date used in this app.","status":"check","src":["tench","sirius"]}]}};
